's one  of the chapters  in the postmodern marks which you might  enjoy  and the further answer is i thought well  what is all this doing there that's just  not just to entertain us  there must be some kind of thread so  i think the thread goes like this it's a  classic  kind of enlightenment argument against  ignorance and superstition and it's  coded um against of  religion uh in general and it does  actually  tie into political economy so the  argument goes like this  okay enlightenment of post-alien  liberals of the time those who've read  david friedrich strauss  on strike on the life of jesus and don't  buy the literal truth of the christian  confessional anymore  we know you're all past that look what  i'm  showing you um is that  the world of commodity production uh  which we're looking at  through empirical testimony and  through works of political economy  is just as mystificatory and just as  performative as we might say these days  that is commodities are as  mark says really strange you just think  there's something in a shop with a price  tag  but let's try to think about them as  really weird  uh how is it that they have a price on  them what does price  mean uh he says prices are the language  that commodities speak to each other  this is getting really strange and  stranger  how is it that we have markets uh with  forces moving by themselves which we  have created  that control our lives in which we bow  down and worship  this is the fetishism of commodities  well  fetishism at the time wasn't freudian uh  it was um the projection of human  qualities on to stone idols  such that they command the humans or  seem to and the humans believe  that the stone idols have magical powers  and money  and monetized behavior uh  and all the things that neo-liberals  complain about  uh and all the boxes we have to take on  the tax forms are just all  instances of being trapped within this  uh performative uh ritualistic  world of commodity concepts that we all  believe in  because we have to but hey look  historically  this was made up historically there was  a time when we didn't have money we  didn't have commodities  we didn't have capital we got on we  traded things we produced things  uh let's not go back there but let's  realize  that commodity production and capitalism  don't have to be here  uh forever so there's a historical  argument going along there along with an  intellectual argument  which is that okay um  woke up liberals you don't believe in  this religious stuff anymore  apply the same argument to the commodity  stuff  it's just as weird as believing in the  incarnation  uh and the holy spirit and the  immaculate conception  it's just it's got dressed up as  political economy  or economics and we don't have to bow  down in worship once we realize  it's a collection of uh human concepts  that we  act out so there is a substantive way  that some people could have recognized  and it's part of the satire  um it's just not uh because it works  both ways i mean it's outlandish to say  the capitalists are werewolves and  capitalism is a vampire sucking the  blood of the workers  but there's loads of empirical stuff put  in there to show exactly that  and marx quotes horrifying stuff by the  time you get  to part eight about what happens in the  indian so-called mutiny  and other things like that from the  mouths you know from the writings of  colonialists and anti-colonies  uh themselves so the whole thing is very  joined up you just have to get all the  way  through to part eight uh to see  how it works where horror meets horror  you have two more questions i'm mindful  of the time people in  the east on the east coast want to get  to dinner and  people in the uk want to get to bed so  let me just give you  a summary of the last two questions one  is from  ali ekdahl who's a student in the  seminar and this follows up on the last  things you were saying what do you think  about the theological rhetoric in  capit